 ### [Point-wise Inverse Discrete Wavelet Transform (PW-IDWT) via Generalized Projection](3394-point-wise-inverse-discrete-wavelet-transform-pw-idwt-via-ge.md)
-The Point-wise Inverse Discrete Wavelet Transform (PW-IDWT) via Generalized Projection (GP) is a method to reconstruct a single signal sample (or a set of samples) from a wavelet decomposition by determining the minimal set of subband coefficients required. It solves the problem of identifying which coefficients at a coarser level contribute to a specific sample at a finer level during synthesis, exploiting the recursive nature of the lifting scheme. Unlike methods that decode entire subbands, this approach calculates the exact "Generalized Projection" of a sample into the transformed domain, minimizing the number of subband images to decode. It builds on [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by providing an efficient, sample-specific inverse transform mechanism.
+The Point-wise Inverse Discrete Wavelet Transform (PW-IDWT) via Generalized Projection (GP) reconstructs a single signal sample (or a set of samples) from a wavelet decomposition by determining the minimal set of subband coefficients required. It solves the problem of identifying which coefficients at a coarser level contribute to a specific sample at a finer level during synthesis, exploiting the recursive nature of the lifting scheme. Unlike methods that decode entire subbands, this approach calculates the exact "Generalized Projection" of a sample into the transformed domain, minimizing the number of subband images to decode. It builds on [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by providing an efficient, sample-specific inverse transform mechanism [G].
 
 ### Wavelet-based Adaptive Resolution Volume Rendering
-A rendering technique that stores reconstructed data at pre-selected wavelet tree nodes based on available storage to reduce reconstruction cost, then traverses the tree per error tolerance to decompress and render blocks in parallel [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Steps:** 1. Traverse the wavelet tree according to a user-specified error tolerance. 2. Identify data blocks of different resolutions that satisfy the error tolerance. 3. Decompress the selected blocks. 4. Render the decompressed blocks in parallel to compose the final image [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Parameters:** Error tolerance: User-specified threshold that controls wavelet tree traversal depth and determines which resolution blocks are decompressed and rendered [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Characteristics:** Reduces run-time communication cost to a minimum and ensures a well-balanced workload among processors when visualizing gigabytes of data with arbitrary error tolerances [S2, unofficial].
