+* **Parameters:** Kernel size $m$: For Haar wavelet, $m=2$; for Daubechies W6, $m=6$ [S3].
+* **Pitfall:** Adjacent blocks have redundant computation because the wavelet kernel extends outside the target block boundary. The extended region width is approximately half the kernel length [S3].
 
 ### On-the-fly Voxel Reconstruction
-A method to reconstruct voxel values on-the-fly from wavelet coefficients during rendering, avoiding pre-computation of the entire volume. It involves finding the appropriate wavelet coefficient at level $k$ and applying inverse wavelet transforms iteratively down to level 0 [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Steps:**
-    1.  Given a wavelet coefficient with $k$ levels and a target voxel $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$, find the voxel (cube of size $1 \times 1 \times 1$) from the level $k$, $w_k$, whose reconstruction contains the target voxel. Map indices $x_0, y_0, z_0$ to $x_k, y_k, z_k$ in $w_k$ by dividing $x_0, y_0, z_0$ by $2^k$. The resultant voxel is in the LLL region of $w_k$ [S3, unofficial].
-    2.  Find the corresponding voxel in the other seven regions of the decomposed data (fHHH, fLLH, fHHL, fHLL, fLHH, fLHL, fLLH, fLLL). To do this, add half of the size of $x_k, y_k$, and $z_k$ (dimensions of $w_k$) to $x_k, y_k$, and $z_k$ respectively [S3, unofficial].
-    3.  Apply the inverse wavelet transformation to the above eight sub-cubes to reconstruct a new sub-cube with size doubled in each axis ($x_{k-1}, y_{k-1}, z_{k-1}$). The new reconstructed sub-cube will be in the LLL of $w_{k-1}$ [S3, unofficial].
-    4.  Repeat steps 2 and 3 until level 0 ($w_0$) is reached. The resultant sub-volume is of size $2^k \times 2^k \times 2^k$ containing the desired voxel $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ [S3, unofficial].
+A method to reconstruct voxel values on-the-fly from wavelet coefficients during rendering, avoiding pre-computation of the entire volume. It involves finding the appropriate wavelet coefficient at level $k$ and applying inverse wavelet transforms iteratively down to level 0 [S3].
+* **Steps:**
+    1. Given a wavelet coefficient with $k$ levels and a target voxel $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$, find the voxel (cube of size $1 \times 1 \times 1$) from the level $k$, $w_k$, whose reconstruction contains the target voxel. Map indices $x_0, y_0, z_0$ to $x_k, y_k, z_k$ in $w_k$ by dividing $x_0, y_0, z_0$ by $2^k$. The resultant voxel is in the LLL region of $w_k$ [S3].
+    2. Find the corresponding voxel in the other seven regions of the decomposed data (fHHH, fLLH, fHHL, fHLL, fLHH, fLHL, fLLH, fLLL). To do this, add half of the size of $x_k, y_k$, and $z_k$ (dimensions of $w_k$) to $x_k, y_k$, and $z_k$ respectively [S3].
+    3. Apply the inverse wavelet transformation to the above eight sub-cubes to reconstruct a new sub-cube with size doubled in each axis ($x_{k-1}, y_{k-1}, z_{k-1}$). The new reconstructed sub-cube will be in the LLL of $w_{k-1}$ [S3].
+    4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until level 0 ($w_0$) is reached. The resultant sub-volume is of size $2^k \times 2^k \times 2^k$ containing the desired voxel $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ [S3].
 
